  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 1201 PM CDT TUE AUG 4 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning reveals a mid to upper level low\nchurning across Central Georgia. Drier air can be seen across \nmuch of Alabama with the exception of a swath of deeper moisture \nwrapping around the low closer to the GA/AL stateline. GOES TPW \nsupports this with PWATs around 1.6-1.7\". This will help maintain\na low to medium (20-50%) chance for showers and storms this \nafternoon/evening and again Wednesday afternoon/evening. By \nThursday, the low will begin to drift north, eventually being \nabsorbed by another approaching upper level trough moving across \nthe Ohio Valley. A more typical summertime pattern sets up the \nweekend with weak flow aloft, leading to diurnally driven showers \nand thunderstorms into early next week.\n\nAs dew point depressions narrow and condensation pressure\ndeficits fall to 3-5mb, there is a 20-45% chance for patchy fog\nearly Wednesday morning . Areas that receive some rain later on\nthis afternoon and evening will have the greatest chance to have \nsome visibility restrictions out the door tomorrow.\n\nTemperatures begin to warm back up through the week with highs\nrebounding into the low to mid 90s tomorrow and continuing\nthrough the weekend. Humid conditions will keep max heat indices\nin the 95-100 degree range which will pose a moderate heat risk\nfor much of Central Alabama.\n\n95/Castillo",
